      "writtenOn": "2024-09-21T09:26:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Trouble is if bpf_skb_pull_data succeeds in pulling sizeof(tcphdr) bytes, but by doing this it pulls from the second segment.\n\nConsider the case of a fragment GSO packet with UDP packets with 1B payload.\n\nWith fraglist, that has one IP + UDP header, either in skb linear or skb frags[], as is true for the 1st byte of payload. Then followed by up to 44 frag_list[] skbs each of 1B.\n\nIf we pull sizeof(struct iphdr) + sizeof(struct tcphdr) then we pull the IP and UDP headers plus 12 payload bytes. This will succeed, but the first 11 frag_list skbs are now pulled in, corrupting the fraglist GSO skb.",
      "revId": "4865bcd842f5c377e593cc1d47c5ba90266d559c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "608ad3a5_5e41d139",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137873
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-21T09:28:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The alternative would be safe:\n\n1. pull the IP header.\n2. depending on iph-\u003eprotocol, pull the UDP or TCP header.\n\nOr slight optimization if expecting lots of UDP traffic (unlikely)\n\n1. pull the IP+UDP header.\n2. if iph-\u003eprotocol says IPPROTO_TCP, pull the remaining TCP header.\n\nBut I understand that you want to optimize for the TCP fast path and only pull once for TCP. Unfortunately I see no way that that is ever safe for UDP traffic.",
      "parentUuid": "ee7c4a09_c62c8a53",
      "revId": "4865bcd842f5c377e593cc1d47c5ba90266d559c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
  ],
